A||N||8||Chuck Essen||Starter Replacement||||1||
Z||000000||Chuck Essen||08-18-2000||08:04 PM||||Starter on 84 2.5 liter 4 cyl manual tranny finally bit the dust. Question is can a novice replace this part easily? Also water pump needs replacement....do these jobs at the same time or no benefit really?  In Sacramento, Special tools needed? Wanna Help?Any advice appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||08-18-2000||08:16 PM||||both of thoes jobs are not to hard to complete, I would do them at the same time just to get them done. I would start with the starter though I have never changed out a starter on my Duke I have replaced the Fuse links (84') so I can tell you not to wasted your time jacking up the car & geting under the car, Just sit on the trunk & lay across the engine bay. The water pump will moast likely be the same but easyer to reach.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierodave||08-18-2000||10:39 PM||||I want to see him change a starter from the top laying over the engine  lol  that would be fun to watch!!!!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.theglobe.com/fierodave/gifs/fierosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Xtreme Fiero's||08-18-2000||11:30 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I say it is better than pulling <BR>it through the exhaust/cat and <BR>droping it on your head  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> and yes it is fun to watch, but it gets done.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Scott_M||08-19-2000||12:05 AM||||Last time I had to replace the starter, I had it up on stands and the old starter sitting next to the car in 30 min. Didn't have to take off the cat. shield off. Just wear goggles and take your time. Make sure you get a good starter. The rebuilt one I got 2 yr ago failed last month. Luckly it had a lifetime warrenty. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||08-20-2000||03:10 PM||||I don't know HOW you managed to install or remove a starter from the top, but it seems to be slightly impossible (at least on my 85 4cyl). The starter is fairly easy, however, I had one problem which I could not fix. The brace that goes on the end of the starter, I could not get it out of the way. No matter what I loosened, it wouldn't turn, and it seemed I had to take everything off that was attached to that. Instead, I just bent it out of the way. Still haven't bent it back though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I don't suppose that is too harmful?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-20-2000||04:30 PM||||replace the brace. It takes allot of mass off the other 2 bolts. If or when it could bite you is imposible to say. If the 2 main bolts start to work loose it can prevent breaking the starter or the 2 bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Chuck Essen||08-20-2000||05:07 PM||||Well lets see....the ogre helps JohnnyK with his problem and I get a lot of conflicting advice. Hey ogre! Revisit and tell me...Is this a 30 min job for a novice? Is a rebuilt OK? Both jobs at once? or take it to a pro?  Thanks again.||||reg||12
Z||000008||JohnnyK||08-20-2000||05:34 PM||||It's a fairly easy job, but it's time consuming trying to get everything twisted out in there and removing the bolts that you can't see (to the solonoid). It's just a pain, but not too hard.||||reg||1
